The provided test class `ChromaClientTests` is attempting to test the `ExecuteHttpRequestAsync` method of the `ChromaClient` class. However, the test is not correctly implemented.

The `ExecuteHttpRequestAsync` method is private, so it cannot be directly tested. To test this method, you can test the public methods of the `ChromaClient` class that call `ExecuteHttpRequestAsync`.

Here's an example of how you can test the `CreateCollectionAsync` method:

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task CreateCollectionAsync_LogsError_WhenHttpRequestFails()
{
    // Arrange
    var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<ChromaClient>>();
    var httpClientMock = new Mock<HttpClient>();
    var collectionName = "TestCollection";

    var chromaClient = new ChromaClient(httpClientMock.Object, "https://example.com", new LoggerFactory().CreateLogger<ChromaClient>());

    // Act and Assert
    await Assert.ThrowsAsync<HttpRequestException>(() => chromaClient.CreateCollectionAsync(collectionName));
    loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(It.IsAny<Exception>(), It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
}
```

This test will test the `CreateCollectionAsync` method and verify that it logs an error when the HTTP request fails.

You can test the other public methods of the `ChromaClient` class in a similar way.

Also, note that the `LoggerFactory` class is not found in the test code. You need to add the necessary using directive or assembly reference to use this class.

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
```

You also need to add the necessary using directive or assembly reference to use the `HttpClient` class.

```csharp
using System.Net.Http;
```
